WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.451
What a language model is.

2
00:00:01.731 --> 00:00:05.784
A language model assigns a probability to a sequence of tokens.

3
00:00:06.064 --> 00:00:24.347
By the chain rule of probability, any distribution over sequences factors exactly as a product of next-token conditionals: P(c1, …, cT) = P(c1) times P(c2 given c1) times … times P(cT given c1 through cT minus 1).

4
00:00:24.627 --> 00:00:27.635
This is not an approximation; it’s an identity.

5
00:00:27.915 --> 00:00:37.387
An autoregressive model, which GPT is, learns those conditionals directly, and generating text means sampling from them one token at a time.

6
00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:41.272
The approximation comes from what you allow each conditional to see.

7
00:00:41.552 --> 00:00:52.496
A bigram model assumes the next character depends only on the current one: P(ct given everything before) is replaced by P(ct given ct minus 1).

8
00:00:52.776 --> 00:00:55.635
That’s a first-order Markov chain over characters.

9
00:00:55.915 --> 00:01:07.541
It is a terrible model of language and a perfect place to learn the whole framework, because you can fit it by counting, check it by hand, and then rebuild it as a neural network and watch both agree.

10
00:01:07.821 --> 00:01:09.784
The dataset is makemore’s names.

11
00:01:10.064 --> 00:01:13.925
txt: 32,033 names, one per line.

12
00:01:14.205 --> 00:01:18.365
Each name becomes a short sequence wrapped in a special token, written “.

13
00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:20.736
”, that marks both the start and the end.

14
00:01:21.016 --> 00:01:22.659
With 26 letters plus “.

15
00:01:22.939 --> 00:01:25.563
”, the vocabulary has 27 tokens.
